@@ -0,0 +1,136 @@
+CREATE and initial file-data authority
+=======================================
+
+Status and conclusion
+--------------------
+
+This note records the qualification of how ``CREATE_FILE`` relates to initial
+READ/WRITE authority for newly created file descriptions on Linux
+7.3.0-rc5. The selected rule is **CREATE authorizes instantiation only**. In a
+confined authority domain, initial data access requires separate, pre-existing
+READ and/or WRITE scope. CREATE neither implies those scopes nor synthesizes
+rights into the resulting file description.
+
+The source change is in ``fs/namei.c``. It extends the prototype already in
+this worktree; it adds no authority right or UAPI. The implementation and test
+results described here were retained in the conversation summary after the
+original ``/tmp`` report and logs were lost. Exact artifacts should therefore
+be treated as historical records, not independently re-verified by this file.
+
+Rule
+----
+
+* A missing-name ``open(O_CREAT)`` needs CREATE authority on the containing
+directory. If the open requests READ and/or WRITE, each requested access also
+needs the corresponding independently scoped right.
+* If the pathname already names an object, ``O_CREAT`` is not an instantiation
+operation and CREATE is not required. Normal READ/WRITE authority still
+applies. ``O_CREAT|O_EXCL`` on an existing entry remains ``EEXIST``.
+* ``O_TRUNC`` is a separate WRITE operation. Truncating an existing inode
+requires WRITE even for ``O_RDONLY|O_TRUNC``. The resulting descriptor remains
+read-only when its access mode is ``O_RDONLY``. For the missing-name route,
+operation access is checked before creation; ``dentry_create()`` includes
+WRITE when deriving access for ``O_TRUNC``.
+* ``O_TMPFILE`` requires CREATE on its parent and separate data scope matching
+the valid requested access mode. Linux rejects ``O_TMPFILE|O_RDONLY`` as an
+invalid combination. A successful write-only tmpfile gets WRITE; a successful
+read/write tmpfile gets READ and WRITE.
+* A successfully opened descriptor is a bearer capability. Removing path
+scopes later does not revoke its access. This rule does not revoke existing
+file descriptions or mappings.
+
+Source reasoning
+----------------
+
+``fs/namei.c:may_o_create()`` is the pre-instantiation path for ordinary
+missing-name ``O_CREAT`` and the atomic-open route. It already carries the
+open operation's access mode. The prototype now checks the prospective child
+path for independent READ/WRITE scope in confined domains, after checking
+parent CREATE and before ``security_inode_create()`` / filesystem
+instantiation.
+
+The important VFS behavior is the ``FMODE_CREATED`` branch in ``do_open()``:
+Linux intentionally sets ``acc_mode = 0`` and clears ``O_TRUNC`` for an inode
+just created by this open. Generic DAC is not re-run on that new inode. The
+``struct file`` access mode was initialized from the open flags, and the
+prototype's initial FD-capability rights derive from that file mode. A CREATE
+check by itself would therefore permit the new descriptor to carry READ or
+WRITE access without a matching data scope. The added check is pre-create,
+not a post-create test that leaves a live, overly powerful descriptor behind.
+
+``vfs_tmpfile()`` is a distinct path and does not use ``may_o_create()``. It
+now checks CREATE on the parent and validates the child-path initial data
+access before calling the filesystem ``->tmpfile`` operation. Both atomic and
+non-atomic ``dentry_create()`` paths also pass requested access; WRITE is
+included for ``O_TRUNC``.
+
+These checks are gated on ``authority_is_confined()``. In the unconfined case
+the new initial-right checks are inert. Existing VFS, mount-write, idmapping,
+filesystem-operation, device-cgroup, and LSM checks continue to run; this
+change does not claim to override them. Filesystem-specific callbacks are not
+substituted by the generic semantic-scope fallback.
+
+Qualification recorded
+----------------------
+
+The retained final QEMU matrix reported zero failures for the creation suite
+and the existing confinement suite. It covered:
+
+* Four READ/WRITE scope masks crossed with named ``O_CREAT`` using
+  ``O_RDONLY``, ``O_WRONLY``, and ``O_RDWR``; missing-name ``O_CREAT|O_TRUNC``;
+  denied creation left no artifact.
+* Existing-file ``O_CREAT`` without CREATE scope, ``O_EXCL``/``EEXIST``, and
+  existing-file ``O_TRUNC`` for write-only and read-only-plus-truncate access.
+* ``open()``, ``openat()``, ``openat2(RESOLVE_IN_ROOT)``, and ``creat()``.
+* ``O_TMPFILE`` write-only/read-write combinations, invalid read-only flags,
+  and a parent outside the CREATE scope.
+* Initial FD-capability bits against ``F_GETFL``; read/write use,
+  ``ftruncate``, ``fallocate``, and mmap behavior; ``F_SETFL`` access-mode
+  immutability; dup/fork/exec and cross-process ``SCM_RIGHTS``; descriptor use
+  after path-scope removal; and restriction of a created RDWR descriptor to
+  READ.
+* Positive scoped creation on rootfs, ramfs, and tmpfs despite parent DAC
+  denial; root ``CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE`` not exceeding handled WRITE; unconfined
+  create compatibility; and ``EROFS`` for named and temporary-file creation
+  on read-only tmpfs.
+
+Retained result counters were:
+
+* ``CREATE_MINTING_FAILURES=0``
+* ``CONFINEMENT_TEST_FAILURES=0``
+* Existing FD-capability, delete, and rename regression counters were each
+  zero against the final recorded kernel image.
+
+The old log and test source are no longer present following the reboot. The
+conversation retained the final image SHA-256 as
+``c0694f3a9a7d70cff6676fb2adafe725e3a7872a67c0b6fe80e3870c7b56d569`` and the
+config SHA-256 as
+``5ab2b496a41786dd90a780c5a063aa93e785351484158d29d28b422f9decd3ea``. The
+recorded build used Debian Trixie tooling, GCC 14.2.0, and GNU ld 2.44. These
+hashes identify artifacts from the prior run; they cannot verify that those
+artifacts still exist.
+
+Known boundary and unqualified areas
+------------------------------------
+
+The guest successfully published an ``O_TMPFILE`` through
+``linkat(..., AT_EMPTY_PATH)`` into a different directory outside the CREATE
+scope. Fresh write-open there was denied by the handled WRITE scope, while the
+original tmpfile descriptor retained its bearer write authority. This was a
+separate namespace mediation gap at the time of this CREATE qualification.
+
+**Follow-up:** the later LINK phase added `FS_LINK` (right ID 7) and exact
+destination-parent enforcement for the audited `link()`/`linkat()` VFS path,
+including `AT_EMPTY_PATH`, when LINK is selected as handled. The historical
+observation remains valid for that older run and for current domains that do
+not mark LINK handled. See the canonical :download:`architecture <../authority/architecture.md>`,
+:download:`design history <../authority/design-history.md>`, and
+:download:`qualification ledger <../authority/qualification-status.md>` for the
+selected source/destination model and current runtime limits.
+
+The original matrix did not qualify ACL entries, idmapped mounts, NFS/FUSE/
+overlayfs or other callback filesystems, race stress, enforcing-LSM policy, or
+every kernel-internal ``dentry_create()`` caller. The ``dentry_create()``
+``O_TRUNC`` access calculation was source-reviewed, not directly runtime
+tested. These results support the bounded initial-creation rule; they do not
+establish complete mediation or a standalone sandbox.
