+Classic DAC compatibility-provider architecture audit
+======================================================
+
+Recovery note
+-------------
+
+The original read-only audit was stored at ``/tmp/authority-dac-audit/report.md``
+and was lost after reboot. This reconstruction uses the retained summary and
+re-checks selected source locations in the current worktree. It preserves the
+original conclusion and scope, but is not a byte-for-byte recovery of the lost
+report. No DAC adapter is implemented by this document or audit.
+
+Conclusion
+----------
+
+A generic Unix DAC compatibility provider is **partially feasible**. Generic
+mode bits, POSIX ACL decisions, idmapped ownership translation, and DAC
+capability overrides can be factored conceptually. A provider cannot safely
+replace the full VFS permission path: it must receive operation and path
+context, preserve ``mnt_idmap`` semantics, and leave filesystem-specific
+``->permission`` callbacks and independent restrictions as fail-closed layers.
+
+The recommended first step was a no-behavior-change boundary for regular-file
+READ/WRITE on generic-permission filesystems. A bounded implementation and
+focused runtime experiment have since been completed; see the follow-up below.
+This does not justify replacing permission decisions or bypassing callbacks.
+
+Current generic DAC and independent checks
+------------------------------------------
+
+In ``fs/namei.c``, ``acl_permission_check()`` implements generic Unix mode and
+POSIX ACL selection, including owner, group, and other cases. It translates
+inode ownership through the mount idmap. ``generic_permission()`` applies this
+mode/ACL result, then accounts for DAC override capabilities such as
+``CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH`` and ``CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE``. The prototype's legacy
+``AUTH_FS_DAC_OVERRIDE`` right is also visible in this generic layer.
+
+``inode_permission_path()`` is the context-aware wrapper in the current tree.
+Its relevant ordering is:
+
+#. In a confined domain, reject a handled semantic operation/access lacking
+   the exact required scoped right.
+#. Apply superblock and inode restrictions, including read-only filesystem
+   ``-EROFS``, immutable-write checks, and unmapped-ID restrictions.
+#. Call the filesystem's ``i_op->permission`` when present; otherwise use
+   ``generic_permission()``.
+#. Only a generic ``-EACCES`` may be satisfied by the operation-aware semantic
+   fallback. A filesystem-specific permission callback is not bypassed.
+#. Apply device-cgroup and LSM inode-permission checks.
+
+This ordering is a key constraint for any provider: semantic authorization
+must not skip mount/superblock, immutable, idmap, filesystem callback,
+devcgroup, or LSM decisions. The callback's error is not equivalent to
+ordinary generic DAC denial.
+
+Operation context and mediation points
+--------------------------------------
+
+A DAC decision is not just a function of inode mode and credentials. Relevant
+context includes the operation (open/read/write/create/search/truncate), the
+pathname or exact parent, mount identity and idmap, open flags, and whether the
+object is new or already exists.
+
+The current tree has operation-aware paths including:
+
+* Path traversal through ``may_lookup()`` and the path-aware permission
+  helpers.
+* Regular-file opens through ``may_open()`` and
+  ``inode_permission_path()`` with access context.
+* Missing-name regular-file creation through ``may_o_create()`` and parent
+  CREATE context.
+* ``O_TMPFILE`` through ``vfs_tmpfile()``, which has separate parent-create
+  behavior.
+* Existing-file ``O_TRUNC`` as an open-time WRITE operation and later
+  truncate checks.
+* Namespace mutation through the prototype's separate create/delete/rename
+  decisions and existing VFS checks.
+
+The sites are not interchangeable. ``FMODE_CREATED`` suppresses the normal
+access-mode DAC check on a just-created inode, so creation must separately
+validate the initial file-description access before instantiation. ``O_TMPFILE``
+does not travel through ``may_o_create()``. Existing-file ``O_CREAT`` must not
+be mistaken for creation. Rename requires its own parent context. These cases
+are why a provider API lacking operation/path context would be unsafe.
+
+A future provider boundary should preserve the VFS caller's existing
+``mnt_idmap`` and distinguish generic DAC denial from errors returned by
+filesystem callbacks. It must not treat all ``-EACCES`` results as
+interchangeable if that would bypass callback, LSM, or mount restrictions.
+
+ACLs, idmapping, and filesystem callbacks
+-----------------------------------------
+
+POSIX ACL evaluation is intertwined with generic permission checking and
+idmapped owner/group interpretation. A provider must preserve ACL precedence
+and the mount's idmap; using raw inode uid/gid or a separate userspace
+approximation would change semantics. The original audit had no retained ACL
+runtime coverage. A later focused provider experiment exercised POSIX ACL
+compatibility and the mandatory ceiling on a test fixture, but idmapped mounts
+and broader ACL/filesystem combinations remain unqualified.
+
+Filesystems may supply ``i_op->permission`` and may have their own open,
+create, or tmpfile behavior. Those callbacks are independent policy and
+filesystem semantics, not generic DAC. The original audit treated them as
+separate fail-closed layers. The prototype's generic path-aware authorization
+does not constitute qualification of NFS, FUSE, overlayfs, or other callback
+filesystems.
+
+DAC capabilities are also operation-sensitive. Read/search override and
+read/write override have different Unix semantics, and inode ownership
+mapping matters. They should not be flattened into an unscoped global allow.
+The prototype's historical/global override behavior was separately recognized
+as a compatibility/security concern; confined handled rights must not be
+exceeded by root's DAC override.
+
+FDs, mappings, and positive grant sources
+-----------------------------------------
+
+An open file description is a bearer capability. Descriptor duplication,
+inheritance, and ``SCM_RIGHTS`` share or transfer an already-open description;
+removing pathname authority does not revoke it. Shared writable mappings may
+outlive changes to namespace/path authority as well. A DAC provider at open
+time cannot retroactively revoke descriptors or mappings.
+
+Positive authorization may come from explicit semantic scope grants, while
+ordinary Unix DAC remains an independent baseline/compatibility mechanism.
+The authorization interface must identify which source granted access and
+must not silently broaden scope through descendants, bind aliases, or an
+ambiguous path identity. Exact-path / mount+dentry identity constraints are
+part of this prototype's model; they are not identical to Unix DAC's
+inode-centric rules.
+
+Known gaps and audit boundaries
+-------------------------------
+
+The retained audit identified the following high-priority caveats:
+
+* Handled semantic rights cover only wired operation classes. Unhandled
+  operations remain ordinary VFS/DAC behavior and are outside a complete
+  confinement claim.
+* Create and later data access are distinct. The ``FMODE_CREATED`` bypass and
+  separate ``O_TMPFILE`` path require explicit qualification of initial FD
+  rights; those were addressed in the follow-up documented in
+  :doc:`create-file-authority`.
+* ``may_o_create()`` has CREATE context, while lower create helpers can use
+  context-free permission checks. Semantic CREATE over DAC denial is therefore
+  not uniformly established across all create paths.
+* The historical tmpfile ``linkat(AT_EMPTY_PATH)`` publication gap is now
+  addressed by the separate handled ``FS_LINK`` right on the exact destination
+  parent for audited ``filename_linkat()`` paths. This is bounded coverage, not
+  complete namespace mediation; callback/context-free link callers and
+  unhandled LINK domains remain outside that claim. See the canonical
+  :download:`qualification status <../authority/qualification-status.md>`.
+* Existing/inherited/imported writable FDs and shared mappings are not
+  revoked. Procfs reopening, hard-link/name import, and imported descriptors
+  require separate treatment.
+* ACL-specific behavior, idmapped mounts, callback filesystems, LSM enforcing
+  behavior, leases/delegations, mount topology corner cases, and race stress
+  were not comprehensively qualified.
+
+These are limitations, not findings that every listed path is exploitable.
+The initial architecture audit was read-only. Later bounded implementation work
+added the narrow fanotify event-FD ceiling described below; it added no rights
+or UAPI and did not broaden the DAC provider.
+
+Feasibility and completed narrow experiment
+-------------------------------------------
+
+**Assessment: partially feasible; generic-permission regular-file opens and
+path-based ``truncate()`` are boundedly qualified.** In ``fs/namei.c``,
+existing regular-file open READ/WRITE requests carry subject credentials,
+object/path, mount idmap, and semantic operation bits. The legacy provider
+calls the existing ``do_inode_permission()`` / ``generic_permission()``
+implementation and classifies ALLOW, DENY, or NOT_APPLICABLE; it does not
+duplicate mode, ACL, ownership, or Linux DAC-capability logic. Generic DAC
+remains the default compatibility source. On generic ``-EACCES``, an explicit
+semantic grant can satisfy the operation; a confined handled right still
+requires its explicit scope. O_RDWR composes READ and WRITE separately when
+the aggregate DAC check denies. Path ``truncate()`` now submits a fresh WRITE
+request through the same bounded path, including when WRITE is unhandled.
+
+The custom ``i_op->permission`` callback path and independent restrictions are
+not bypassed. Overlayfs is an example: its callback checks overlay DAC and
+lower-layer permission under mounter credentials, so the provider does not
+replace callback failures. Managed mmap is a separate FD-capability decision,
+not a DAC-provider extension; tests verify shared-write denial after FD
+attenuation and prevention of a later ``mprotect()`` upgrade. ``rw_verify_area()``
+checks managed FD READ/WRITE rights after the LSM file-permission hook and is
+used by ordinary and io_uring I/O plus representative sendfile/splice/copy
+paths. ``F_SETFL`` only changes mutable status flags, not ``f_mode`` or
+``O_ACCMODE``; a runtime attempt did not restore an attenuated READ right.
+``ftruncate()`` and existing-FD I/O remain established-capability operations.
+
+The clean external build and exact isolated QEMU/KVM image reported 47 focused
+integration cases with zero failures, including explicit path-truncate grants
+and denials, ftruncate distinction, path/proc reopen, renamed-scope paths,
+mmap/mprotect, and FD-capability-gated ``sendfile``, ``splice``, and
+``copy_file_range``, including an ``F_SETFL`` non-escalation case. Reconstructed
+regression subsets reported CREATE 3,
+LINK 4, DELETE 2, RENAME 2, focused confinement 34, and FD capability 6, each
+with zero failures. These are bounded subsets, not restoration or rerun of the
+complete historical suites. Exact hashes, logs, and harness are temporary in
+``/tmp/authority-dac-integration/``.
+
+Source review found an exceptional XFS ``open_by_handle`` fresh-description
+path that bypasses ``may_open()``. The exported ceiling helper denies missing
+handled READ/WRITE scope there; the ioctl still requires ``CAP_SYS_ADMIN`` and
+does not use the generic DAC provider. The follow-up isolated QEMU run tested
+both XFS-specific and generic ``open_by_handle_at()`` READ/WRITE grants and
+missing-scope denials on a compatible XFS v5 fixture. Ordinary namei open
+variants and io_uring openat/openat2 converge on the normal open path; PIDFD
+and namespace export opens are special-object paths.
+
+A separate capability-establishment follow-up distinguished aliases/imports
+from fresh descriptions. ``pidfd_getfd()`` imports the target's existing
+``struct file`` after ptrace-style authorization; runtime testing confirmed
+that unrestricted source rights survive receiver path denial while READ-only
+and zero-right source attenuation also survives. ``/proc/<pid>/fd/N`` reopen
+is instead a fresh open and was denied by the receiver's handled scope after
+the ptrace precondition passed. io_uring OPENAT followed namei authorization;
+fixed-file registration and update preserved a later FD-rights reduction.
+``O_PATH`` provided object/path reachability but not READ access; a fresh
+procfs reopen was denied.
+
+Runtime falsification exposed one additional fresh-description path:
+fanotify event-FD creation used ``dentry_open_nonotify()`` without the handled
+regular-file READ/WRITE ceiling. The ordinary open control failed with
+``EACCES`` while the fanotify listener could read the event FD. A narrow
+ceiling check is now applied in ``create_fd()`` before that open; the postpatch
+``FAN_REPORT_FD_ERROR`` test reported ``-EACCES``. This adds no DAC-provider
+route and retains the existing file-open LSM/filesystem checks.
+
+Anonymous storage was kept separate from persistent pathname files.
+``memfd_create()`` returns an unlinked tmpfs-backed regular inode and
+``F_GETFD_CAP`` reported READ|WRITE; a confined guest with handled READ|WRITE
+and no scopes successfully wrote and shared-mapped it. SysV shared-memory
+write likewise succeeded. These are explicit out-of-path-model boundaries,
+not resolved by this generic-DAC experiment. No new rights or UAPI were added.
+
+**Narrow verdict:** the regular-file generic-DAC provider is supported only
+for ordinary generic-permission opens plus path-based ``truncate()``. It is
+not a universal fresh-open or authorization core. Do not extend it based on
+these runs. Callback filesystems, idmapped mounts, enforcing LSM, races,
+broader filesystems, immutable/append-only cases, and the complete historical
+prototype regression suites remain unqualified. The exact follow-up image,
+config, QEMU logs, and capability harness are temporary under
+``/tmp/authority-capability-establishment/``; see
+``Documentation/authority/qualification-status.md`` and
+``/tmp/authority-capability-establishment/report.md``.
