+Historical vulnerability review: recovery record
+==================================================
+
+Recovery limitation
+-------------------
+
+The original report and downloaded source material under
+``/tmp/authority-cve-eval/`` were lost after reboot. The surviving design
+history supplies an approximate CVE list and classifications, but not the
+advisories, upstream commit IDs, evidence provenance, or per-CVE reachability
+analysis. This document does not fabricate those missing citations and is not
+a substitute for the original evidence-backed report. See
+``Documentation/authority/design-history.md`` and
+``Documentation/authority/qualification-status.md`` for the retained
+approximate labels, explicitly marked unverified.
+
+Scope and method retained
+-------------------------
+
+The prior evaluation compared historical vulnerability records, vendor
+advisories, and upstream fixes against the prototype's source-level mediation
+and its bounded runtime behavior. The report distinguished:
+
+* what a vulnerability's affected operation/path was;
+* whether that operation was covered by a prototype hook;
+* whether a missing hook represented likely reachability, or only a
+  source-level coverage gap; and
+* what runtime experiments established on the fixed, patched test kernel.
+
+No vulnerable kernel was booted and no historical exploit was run. Runtime
+tests used synthetic fixtures in isolated QEMU/KVM guests. The purpose was
+coverage analysis, not exploit reproduction.
+
+Retained findings
+-----------------
+
+The prior report concluded that prototype coverage was incomplete and that a
+source-level gap must not automatically be described as a reachable CVE. The
+prototype covers only a bounded set of semantic filesystem operations; it is
+not a complete mediation layer. Existing file descriptions and mappings are
+not revoked, some namespace/import operations are not wired, and
+filesystem-specific behavior and LSM policy were not fully qualified.
+
+The historical assessment explicitly separated the prototype's coverage from
+the vulnerability's actual reachability and from exploit reproduction. It did
+not claim that synthetic tests reproduced any historical vulnerability.
+
+The more detailed qualification subsequently retained in the project notes
+found additional concrete boundaries: CREATE must not mint initial READ/WRITE
+FD access (see :doc:`create-file-authority`), and tmpfile publication through
+``linkat(AT_EMPTY_PATH)`` into a different directory was possible even though
+fresh writes there remained blocked by the handled WRITE scope. Those findings
+are not retroactively assigned to any particular CVE here because the original
+CVE-to-operation mapping is unavailable. A subsequent LINK phase added a
+handled exact-destination-parent right for the audited hard-link paths; this
+does not change what the older runtime reported. See the canonical
+:download:`qualification status <../authority/qualification-status.md>` for
+the current bounded result and remaining gaps.
+
+What is missing / how to restore the original review
+----------------------------------------------------
+
+The following evidence did not survive and must be reacquired before citing
+this as a source-backed CVE-by-CVE analysis:
+
+* affected version ranges and exact source/advisory references for each retained CVE ID;
+* links or copies of NVD/CVE records, vendor advisories, and upstream fixes;
+* the original per-CVE prototype-coverage and reachability conclusions;
+* the exact source references and evidence provenance; and
+* the original runtime fixtures and logs.
+
+To restore the full report, repeat the source/advisory research from trusted
+public records, record retrieval dates and stable URLs/commit IDs, map each
+vulnerability's preconditions to the current source, and label source-level
+coverage separately from runtime evidence. Do not run historical exploits or
+boot a known-vulnerable kernel. The bounded synthetic QEMU policy remains in
+force.
+
+This recovery note is intentionally incomplete. It preserves the safety
+constraints and aggregate conclusions without presenting unverifiable
+per-CVE claims as fact.
