+"Source-reviewed" must never silently become "runtime-qualified."
+
+---
+
+# No complete sandbox claim
+
+Even after mandatory confinement, the project is **not currently a complete process sandbox**.
+
+The strongest justified claim is closer to:
+
+> An opt-in irreversible mandatory authority domain is implemented and qualified for a defined subset of semantic filesystem operations.
+
+Do not claim:
+
+```text
+all filesystem operations are mediated
+
+all Linux authority is unified
+
+complete sandbox
+
+complete DAC replacement
+
+complete LSM replacement
+```
+
+---
+
+# Important design non-goals
+
+Document at least:
+
+```text
+do not replace every Linux security mechanism
+
+do not remove UID/GID ownership
+
+do not make every process use explicit authority
+
+do not make LSMs obsolete
+
+do not make namespaces/cgroups/seccomp obsolete
+
+do not claim kernel-bug containment
+
+do not treat path strings as stable object authority
+
+do not silently revoke every pre-existing FD
+
+do not compose semantically different rights merely because Unix DAC uses the same mode bit
+```
+
+---
+
+# Complementary Linux mechanisms
+
+The project is intended to provide one common semantic authority substrate for a class of filesystem authority decisions.
+
+Other mechanisms remain complementary:
+
+```text
+seccomp:
+    syscall filtering
+
+cgroups:
+    resource governance
+
+namespaces:
+    system-view / resource isolation
+
+LSMs:
+    mandatory policy
+
+traditional DAC:
+    compatibility/default Unix policy
+
+explicit authority:
+    fine-grained operational delegation/confinement
+```
+
+Do not present the prototype as replacing all of these.
+
+---
+
+# Key invariants section
+
+Create a prominent concise invariant list.
+
+It should include at least:
+
+### Identity
+
+```text
+identity != authority
+```
+
+### Monotonic process authority
+
+\[
+A_{future} \subseteq A_{current}
+\]
+
+within one authority lineage.
+
+### Monotonic FD derivation
+
+\[
+FD_{child} \subseteq FD_{parent}
+\]
+
+### No local restoration
+
+Removed authority cannot be recreated from the weaker capability/domain itself.
+
+### Object-backed scope
+
+Authority attaches to resolved object/mount identity, not permanent pathname spelling.
+
+### Established capability persistence
+
+Dropping pathname authority does not retroactively revoke intentionally retained established FDs.
+
+### Data != namespace
+
+```text
+READ/WRITE != CREATE/DELETE/RENAME/LINK
+```
+
+### CREATE does not mint data authority
+
+\[
+CREATE \not\Rightarrow READ
+\]
+
+\[
+CREATE \not\Rightarrow WRITE
+\]
+
+inside confinement.
+
+### Multi-target operations require conjunction
+
+For cross-parent rename:
+
+\[
+Rename(A,B)
+=
+Authority(A)
+\land
+Authority(B)
+\]
+
+### Namespace removal != object revocation
+
+Unlinking an object does not invalidate already-established file capabilities.
+
+### Fresh authority establishment is checked
+
+An attenuated handle must not regain removed authority merely by reopening the same object through another name when mandatory mediation covers the requested operation.
+
+### Independent restrictions remain authoritative
+
+Explicit authority does not bypass LSM denials, EROFS, immutable state, or other unrelated structural restrictions.
+
+### Conventional privilege cannot widen confinement
+
+UID 0 / DAC-bypass capabilities must not exceed the handled mandatory authority ceiling.
