+# Design history: filesystem authority and FD capabilities
+
+This is the decision record, not a claim that every historical choice remains fully implemented. **Historical** means an earlier model/test; **current** means checked against the worktree described in [architecture.md](architecture.md); **runtime-reported** means the former isolated-QEMU run reported it, but its `/tmp` logs/test sources were lost after reboot. For present evidence limits see [qualification-status.md](qualification-status.md).
+
+## 1. Starting thesis: identity is not authority
+
+The prototype began from dissatisfaction with treating a Unix identity as a broad bundle of recoverable filesystem access. The motivating case was Alice running a worker as Alice: the worker should be able to operate on project A without thereby recovering all DAC access to project B, private SSH keys, and documents. Ownership and UID/GID remain useful for accounting, audit, compatibility, and default policy; they should not force a process to retain operational authority.
+
+The early system exposed broad `AUTH_NORMAL` / `AUTH_SYSTEM` modes and a control interface. The first proof-of-concept proposition was that a task could keep ordinary UID/GID/fsuid/fsgid and zero Linux effective/permitted capabilities while a separate authority mechanism enabled selected filesystem access; created objects still had ordinary Unix ownership. This identity-separation result was historically QEMU-tested, but is not reproducible from the lost harnesses in the current checkout.
+
+`AUTH_SYSTEM` is still present in the current source as privileged PoC/compatibility scaffolding. It grants a global generic-DAC override, not a polished production policy. It is explicitly rejected as the final fine-grained model. **Current-source caveat:** an unconfined caller with `CAP_SYS_ADMIN` can reacquire this broad PoC authority after clearing it with `AUTH_NORMAL`; therefore the monotonic principle is not enforced across privileged pre-confinement transitions. Confinement rejects `AUTH_SYSTEM` and cannot be cleared through the PoC control path.
+
+## 2. Representation and scope evolution
+
+### Fixed mask versus extensible rights
+
+**Rejected:** hard-code semantic rights into a fixed 64-bit mask.
+
+**Chosen:** dynamically sized bitmaps in refcounted authority objects. The work intentionally used a right at bit 80 (`AUTH_FS_TEST_RIGHT_80`) to catch accidental `u64` assumptions. Current `kernel/authority.c` still has this sentinel and 81-bit bitmap width. UAPI requests are size/versioned variable-length bitmaps; unknown set bits fail closed and zero-filled extension space is accepted in the current confinement parser.
+
+The right identifiers are a small PoC filesystem vocabulary, not a general mature authority-class registry. Adding a bit above 64 proves representation extensibility only; it does not prove a sustainable UAPI policy taxonomy.
+
+### Path strings versus object-backed scope
+
+**Rejected:** persistent string-prefix rules such as `allow /workspace/*`.
+
+**Why:** renames, symlink traversal, `..`, replacement at an old spelling, mount aliases, and bind mounts make string ancestry unstable and ambiguous.
+
+**Chosen:** pin a resolved `struct path` and compare mount+dentry identity. Current `authority_path_in_scope()` uses same-mount dentry ancestry for most rights; DELETE and RENAME use exact parent identity. Historically tested behaviors included authority following rename of the scoped object, not transferring to a replacement at the old pathname, symlink spelling not defining the grant, and bind-mount aliases not automatically inheriting a mount-specific scope. Exact tests/logs are lost; see qualification status.
+
+This is not equivalent to Unix DAC's inode-oriented ownership model. It is an intentional capability-scope identity choice whose behavior through unusual filesystems and disconnected dentries remains a qualification topic.
+
+## 3. Semantic operation vocabulary
+
+### CREATE separated from directory WRITE
+
+A distinct `CREATE_FILE` right was introduced so object instantiation would not be a synonym for generic directory WRITE/DAC override. Historical tests checked that CREATE by itself did not imply reading/writing existing files, mkdir, deletion, or rename. The right is intended for regular-file creation paths and a parent directory scope.
+
+An early ambiguity was whether a successful `O_CREAT|O_WRONLY` open should be treated as “CREATE establishing its initial handle.” That resembles traditional Unix semantics and was initially left open. The later resolution is Model B: inside confinement, CREATE does not mint initial data rights. See §10.
+
+### TRAVERSE and handle-first lookup
+
+**Rejected:** let a semantic traverse grant transparently authorize arbitrary ancestor walk before the final object is known. That risks exposing unrelated sibling reachability while resolving absolute or ambient paths.
+
+**Chosen design direction:** bounded, handle-first resolution such as `openat2()` with `RESOLVE_BENEATH` or `RESOLVE_IN_ROOT`, using a directory FD and a constrained path. Historical synthetic tests covered valid traverse+operation combinations and rejected `..`, deep-`..`, symlink escapes, wrong-parent FDs, and mount aliases.
+
+**Current-source correction:** `FS_TRAVERSE` exists and is consulted in `may_lookup()` paths, but the current `PR_CAPAUTH_CONFINE` parser does not allow it to be marked mandatory/handled. Thus the intended bounded traversal design is not a current mandatory-confinement guarantee. Some lookup fast paths also deserve direct source/runtime requalification. Do not promote the historical intended semantics to a current full boundary.
+
+### READ and WRITE separated
+
+`READ_FILE` and `WRITE_FILE` were added as independent data establishment/access rights. The intended open mapping is:
+
+```text
+O_RDONLY -> READ
+O_WRONLY -> WRITE
+O_RDWR   -> READ + WRITE
+```
+
+Historical tests covered explicit access despite mode-000 DAC objects, rejection when the needed right/scope was absent, and independence of READ from WRITE, CREATE, truncate, append, namespace creation/removal, and rename. A writable descriptor established while scope is present remains a bearer capability after the path scope is dropped; this is intentional and led to the FD capability distinction below.
+
+### DELETE separated from WRITE
+
+**Rejected:** treat file-data WRITE as permission to unlink.
+
+**Reason:** unlink changes a name in a parent directory; it does not write file contents. A file's data scope should not authorize namespace removal.
+
+**Chosen:** `DELETE_FILE` is attached to the exact parent directory, not inherited from an ancestor scope. Historical qualification included wrong-parent denial, no implication from victim READ/WRITE or CREATE/RENAME, unlinking a symlink name without deleting its target, removing only one hardlink name, and existing FDs surviving unlink. `rmdir` is not modeled by the current regular-entry delete right. Current `PR_CAPAUTH_UNLINKAT` is a bounded direct-child, non-directory operation.
+
+### RENAME as a distinct multi-object operation
+
+**Rejected:** compose `DELETE(source) + CREATE(destination)` into rename.
+
+**Reason:** rename is atomic and has replacement/exchange/topology rules unlike a sequence of independent delete and create operations.
+
+**Chosen:** `FS_RENAME` is an independent right on both exact parent directories:
+
+\[
+Rename(A,B) = RENAME(A) \land RENAME(B)
+\]
+
+Historical tests covered source-parent-only denial, destination-parent-only denial, both-parent success, and that CREATE/DELETE/data rights did not substitute. Replacement of an existing destination remains part of atomic rename; existing FDs to the replaced inode continue to refer to it.
+
+**Current boundary:** `PR_CAPAUTH_RENAMEAT2` is direct-child, regular-file, two-dirfd and `flags == 0`. The ordinary `renameat2` path separately reaches `vfs_rename_internal()` with parent context; directory and nonzero-flag variants were not runtime-qualified and are not promised by the bounded PoC API. Independent sticky, immutable, mount, LSM, delegation, lease, and filesystem rules are expected to remain effective.
+
+## 4. File descriptors become explicit capabilities
+
+### Audit of established file semantics
+
+Source inspection showed that Linux often checks access when a file description is established, then later read/write/ftruncate/fallocate/mmap paths trust a combination of `f_mode`, file operations, and security hooks. `F_SETFL` cannot change `O_ACCMODE`. This makes an established `struct file` a bearer capability for some operations, but not blanket authority over metadata or namespace: chmod/chown/xattrs, ioctl, and link/unlink have distinct checks and semantics.
+
+**Lesson:** pathname authority and FD authority are different state. Requiring path scope on every later read would contradict established open-file semantics, while allowing arbitrary FD operations would mislabel all object authority as data access.
+
+### Restriction versus derivation; dup versus clone
+
+The PoC added `F_GETFD_CAP`, `F_RESTRICTFD_CAP`, and `F_CLONEFD_CAP`.
+
+* Restriction changes current capability rights to `old & requested`; there is no re-enable operation.
+* `dup`, `dup2`, `dup3`, and `F_DUPFD*` mean another reference to the same open file description. Rights attenuation is shared.
+* `F_CLONEFD_CAP` creates an independent file description from a pinned source path with rights intersected against source rights. It is not a pathname reopen through `/proc`; offsets subsequently diverge.
+
+The first implementation supports only regular non-anonymous files on `rootfs`, `ramfs`, and `tmpfs`. It refuses paths/backing/unsupported file objects; clone also rejects selected file-owner/async state. Generic ioctl semantics are not guessed: managed-FD ioctl handling is deliberately narrow/fail-closed.
+
+### SCM_RIGHTS and inherited descriptors
+
+An FD passed with `SCM_RIGHTS` is explicit delegation of that established capability. Passing an attenuated clone gives the recipient the weaker capability; passing the original gives the recipient the original authority. Fork/exec also inherit descriptors according to ordinary FD rules and close-on-exec state. Confinement cannot erase these safely without a separately designed revocation model.
+
+## 5. Additive authority failed as a sandbox
+
+The first semantic-authority design was additive:
+
+\[
+Allow = DAC \lor ExplicitAuthority
+\]
+
+It made selected positive grants possible, but did not prevent traditional DAC grants outside semantic scopes. A synthetic QEMU test used a UID-1000 worker with a narrow workspace scope and a separate UID-owned file. Ordinary write open of the outside file still succeeded. This was not a corner-case bypass; it invalidated the assumption that “no semantic right” meant “deny.”
+
+### `/proc/self/fd` was a symptom, not the root cause
+
+A read-only cloned FD correctly rejected `write(fd)`. Reopening `/proc/self/fd/N` with `O_WRONLY` could nevertheless obtain a fresh writable description when DAC independently permitted it.
+
+**Rejected:** special-case `/proc/self/fd` as the fix. The same authority could be recovered through another pathname, symlink, hard link, rename, or other independent grant source.
+
+**Lesson:** FD attenuation only attenuates derivation from that FD; it does not remove ambient authority the process holds elsewhere.
+
+## 6. Mandatory domains added
+
+The additive failure motivated opt-in irreversible confinement. For selected handled operations, explicit domain rights form a ceiling that ordinary DAC and root's DAC-bypass capabilities cannot exceed. Explicit scoped grants can still authorize selected operations that generic DAC denies, subject to independent LSM/filesystem/structural checks.
+
+This is not simply `DAC AND deny-only sandbox`: semantic authority is a positive grant source in addition to a mandatory ceiling. The source audit found the decision flow distributed through `fs/namei.c`, not one central policy hook.
+
+Current `PR_CAPAUTH_CONFINE` (checked in `kernel/sys.c`) requires a single-threaded caller, accepts only `CREATE_FILE`, `READ_FILE`, `WRITE_FILE`, `DELETE_FILE`, `RENAME`, and `LINK` in its handled mask, refuses the legacy global DAC override, commits the confined credential and sets `no_new_privs`. Scope addition and `AUTH_SYSTEM` are disallowed after confinement; reductions remain possible. Current `authority_cred_clear()` will not clear a confined object on privileged-exec credential transition. This is the current code shape, not proof that all channels are mediated.
+
+The process-domain ceiling does **not** revoke existing/imported file descriptions or mappings. It also does not currently make TRAVERSE, directory operations, metadata, ioctl, or all filesystems mandatory. LINK has since been added to the handled mask, but only the audited hard-link VFS paths are covered. “Irreversible” means the domain cannot be exited/widened through the implemented PoC control flow; it does not mean every task authority is represented in that domain.
+
+## DAC remains compatibility policy; a narrow provider experiment follows
+
+A later architectural fork asked whether the prototype should remove classic DAC or eagerly translate every DAC-granted object into an explicit capability set. Both directions were rejected.
+
+* **Do not remove UID/GID, inode owners, mode bits, ACLs, chmod/chown, umask, or quota/accounting.** Ownership remains useful and ordinary applications need compatibility/default policy. The target is to separate a confined process's effective authority from what its identity could otherwise recover.
+* **Do not eagerly enumerate DAC-accessible objects into capabilities.** chmod/chown, ACL edits, newly created files, mount changes, and idmapped mounts make a materialized list stale and unscalable. If DAC becomes a provider, evaluate current subject/object/operation context lazily.
+* **Do not translate `rwx` one-to-one into semantic rights.** For a directory, traditional `w+x` participates in create, unlink, rename, and link decisions. A provider must answer whether DAC authorizes this semantic operation, not merely inspect one global bit.
+* **Do not treat every positive grant mechanism as a deny layer.** DAC, DAC-bypass capabilities, semantic scopes, and imported FDs can turn an operation into allow. LSM denials, `EROFS`, and structural restrictions normally add denials. The former sources threaten a common upper-bound model if left ambient; the latter should remain independent.
+
+At that audit checkpoint, the source had an **Authority State Core** (rights, scopes, matching were relatively centralized) but no shared operation-state contract or universal **Authorization Decision Core** (VFS decisions remained distributed). It judged a generic Unix mode/ACL/DAC-capability provider **partially feasible** only if it receives operation/path/`mnt_idmap` context and does not bypass custom `i_op->permission`, mount, devcgroup, or LSM checks. A bounded regular-file READ/WRITE open provider was then qualified on synthetic QEMU fixtures. A later integration checkpoint extended path-based `truncate()` to the same bounded generic-permission decision, enforced managed-FD mmap ceilings through `mprotect()`, and source-reviewed alternate fresh-open routes. It found XFS `open_by_handle` bypasses `may_open()`; that privileged route now enforces the handled semantic ceiling, but does not use the generic DAC provider. Callback filesystems remain fail-closed and unqualified. The exact integration run passed 47 focused cases, but reconstructed CREATE/confinement/FD/DELETE/RENAME subsets are not the unavailable complete historical suites. Verdict remains deliberately narrow: generic-permission regular-file opens and path truncate only; no universal decision service or provider extension. The later minimal request-classification core is described in §13 and does not broaden this provider verdict. See [dac-architecture-audit.rst](../authority-prototype/dac-architecture-audit.rst) and [qualification status](qualification-status.md).
+
+A vocabulary-ordering consequence follows: the known hard-link publication ambiguity is now specified and boundedly qualified, but mkdir/rmdir, symlink creation, metadata mutation, context-free filesystem callers, and other namespace operations remain outside this LINK right. Resolve any operation that the future provider would classify before centralizing an incomplete semantic vocabulary.
+
+### Capability-establishment follow-up
+
+The later source/runtime audit separated fresh descriptions from references to existing file objects. Same-UID `pidfd_getfd()` imports the target's existing file reference after ptrace-style permission checks; the receiver gets the source file's current bearer rights, not a new path authorization. By contrast, `/proc/<pid>/fd/N` reopening, `O_PATH` data reopen, generic handle-open, and XFS handle-open are fresh descriptions. Ordinary io_uring openat follows namei, while fixed-file registration/update preserves the same file object's monotone FD restrictions.
+
+Runtime falsification found that privileged fanotify event-FD creation bypassed `may_open()` and could deliver a readable regular-file FD after an ordinary READ open was denied. A narrow handled READ/WRITE ceiling was added to `fanotify_user.c:create_fd()`; the post-change isolated guest returned `-EACCES` in a `FAN_REPORT_FD_ERROR` event. This does not extend the generic DAC provider. Generic and XFS handle opens were runtime-tested on compatible XFS v5 fixtures; both retained missing-scope denials and scoped grants, with their distinct Linux privilege gates intact.
+
+`memfd_create()` and SysV shared memory were separately classified as anonymous/in-memory storage, not pathname filesystem authority. Runtime tests showed they can establish unscoped writable memory while filesystem READ/WRITE are handled; this remains an explicit boundary, not a new semantic right or claim of confinement. Any attempt to govern these routes needs a separate decision about anonymous storage semantics. See [qualification status](qualification-status.md) for bounded evidence and non-goals.
+
+## 7. Historical CVE/security evaluation changed the claim
+
+A source/advisory review compared selected historical vulnerabilities with the additive model. No vulnerable kernel or historical exploit was run; the evaluation used records/advisories/upstream fixes and synthetic fixtures. Its key architectural lesson was not “the prototype prevents CVEs,” but that unmodified software can continue using authority paths the prototype does not intercept.
+
+The retained historical classifications, supplied in the surviving design record but not revalidated here against the lost report, included:
+
+* `CVE-2021-41773` Apache traversal — **UNAFFECTED as shipped**; a bounded reconstruction could reject escape, but ordinary Apache was not forced through it.
+* `CVE-2023-22809` sudoedit, `CVE-2021-3156` sudo, `CVE-2021-4034` PwnKit, and `CVE-2019-5736` runc — **UNAFFECTED** by the additive filesystem prototype.
+* `CVE-2022-0847` Dirty Pipe and `CVE-2016-5195` Dirty COW — **UNAFFECTED / data-integrity gap** at the authority layer.
+* `CVE-2022-0185` filesystem-context kernel memory corruption and `CVE-2021-22555` netfilter kernel memory corruption — **OUT_OF_MODEL** (kernel compromise is outside the authority abstraction).
+* `CVE-2018-1002105` Kubernetes API authorization — **OUT_OF_MODEL** for this filesystem-authority scope.
+
+These are retained approximate labels, not independently evidence-checked claims in this reconstruction. Original IDs/evidence files were lost; see [historical-cve-review.rst](../authority-prototype/historical-cve-review.rst). Definitions matter: PREVENTED means the relevant primitive is unavailable; CONTAINED means a bug can trigger but delegated impact is bounded; UNAFFECTED means the relevant original path is not stopped; OUT_OF_MODEL means the trust boundary is outside this mechanism.
+
+The historical evaluation and `/proc` result caused the architecture to evolve:
+
+```text
+positive semantic grants
+  -> DAC access outside scopes observed
+  -> FD reopen recovery observed
+  -> additive model rejected as confinement
+  -> mandatory domains for selected operations
+```
+
+## 8. Creation can mint a capability: another changed assumption
+
+The later DAC audit traced `FMODE_CREATED`: Linux suppresses the normal open access check for a file just instantiated by that open; the new `struct file` still has the requested access mode, and subsequent I/O trusts the description. This raised the explicit question: may CREATE implicitly mint READ/WRITE?
+
+**Candidate A:** CREATE includes the initial O_RDONLY/O_WRONLY/O_RDWR description, close to ordinary Unix behavior.
+
+**Chosen candidate B for confined domains:** CREATE authorizes instantiation only; independent READ/WRITE scope must authorize initial FD mode. A post-create denial was rejected because it could leave an unauthorized object or descriptor behind. The check is before instantiation in `may_o_create()`; `O_TMPFILE` receives separate handling in `vfs_tmpfile()`; `dentry_create()` routes are source-adjusted too. Unconfined behavior remains ordinary Linux behavior.
+
+A later isolated guest matrix reported zero failures across named open APIs, O_TMPFILE, scope combinations, FD-cap rights and existing-file `O_TRUNC`. Exact logs/harness were in `/tmp` and are gone. The detailed recovered note is [create-file-authority.rst](../authority-prototype/create-file-authority.rst).
+
+## 9. O_TMPFILE revealed CREATE is not LINK
+
+The creation/data rule did not authorize namespace publication. The retained historical QEMU result observed successful `linkat(..., AT_EMPTY_PATH)` publication of an O_TMPFILE into a different parent outside CREATE scope; fresh write-open there was still denied by WRITE confinement. At that time this demonstrated:
+
+```text
+object instantiation != namespace insertion
+```
+
+This phase adds the bounded `FS_LINK` right (ID 7) and makes it selectable as handled. The exact historical bypass succeeds when LINK is not selected as handled, as expected for an unhandled VFS operation; with LINK handled, publication without the exact destination-parent scope now returns `EACCES`. This closes the tested namespace-insertion gap at the semantic authorization layer, not through an O_TMPFILE-specific prohibition.
+
+### Candidate source/destination models
+
+* **A — destination only, source is mere reachability:** simple and naturally handles `AT_EMPTY_PATH`, but too imprecise as a whole policy statement: source selectors and legacy source-eligibility rules still matter. Destination authority alone is not sufficient to invoke the syscall.
+* **B — explicit LINK on source object and destination parent:** expresses “permission to expose” plus “permission to mutate namespace,” but requires an object-scope representation for regular files and anonymous tmpfiles, new source authorization semantics, and a ruleset for pathname versus FD sources. The VFS already has distinct source lookup/FD and protected-hardlink checks; adding another source right was not justified by this qualification.
+* **C — exact destination LINK plus normal source resolution/eligibility:** **selected.** LINK controls the namespace mutation. For pathname sources Linux lookup plus `may_linkat()` remains authoritative; for `AT_EMPTY_PATH`, an established FD supplies the object selector subject to the ordinary open-credential / `CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH` guard and `may_linkat()`. No READ/WRITE capability bit or separate semantic source scope is required to make the new name.
+* **D — separate `PUBLISH_OBJECT(source)` and `LINK_INTO(parent)`:** deferred. It may be warranted if future policy needs per-object exposure authority beyond normal Linux hard-link eligibility, but it is not the minimum model supported by current evidence.
+
+The selected model is destination-parent authority **and** a valid Linux source route, not destination-only permission in isolation. `FS_LINK` does not bypass protected-hardlink ownership/safety, source path reachability, FD credential restrictions, filesystem checks, or LSM policy.
+
+### Exact scope and operation semantics
+
+`FS_LINK` is an exact `(mount,dentry)` destination-parent right, parallel to the namespace scope identity already used by DELETE/RENAME. It follows the pinned parent object after rename, does not transfer to a replacement at the prior pathname, and does not cross a bind-mount alias. It does not compose with CREATE, DELETE, RENAME, READ, or WRITE. The VFS still rejects directory hard links and existing destination names; link count and filesystem behavior remain independent.
+
+`filename_linkat()` carries the resolved destination parent into `vfs_link_path()`. The existing context-free `vfs_link()` entry point fails closed when the current domain marks LINK handled, because it cannot prove destination mount+dentry identity. This intentionally makes kernel callers without operation-path context unable to link in such a domain; those callback/filesystem paths remain unqualified.
+
+Source-side details matter:
+
+* `link()` and pathname `linkat()` resolve the source through ordinary lookup; `AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW` selects the followed target, while the default links the symlink object itself.
+* `AT_EMPTY_PATH` uses the file's pinned path, but Linux rejects a foreign `f_cred` unless the caller has `CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH` in that credential's user namespace. `SCM_RIGHTS` transfers the FD but does not erase that gate.
+* When `fs.protected_hardlinks` is enabled, `may_linkat()` still requires inode ownership/`CAP_FOWNER` or a safe regular source (not setuid, not executable setgid, and generic READ|WRITE permitted). The semantic grant does not bypass this independent source policy.
+* Data-FD mode bits are not consulted by the LINK hook. Runtime tests linked using READ-only, WRITE-only, O_PATH, and zero-right cloned source FDs; same-credential SCM_RIGHTS transfer also worked. A foreign-credential broker FD failed `AT_EMPTY_PATH` with `ENOENT` as normal Linux policy requires.
+
+Thus a worker with destination LINK authority can publish a source FD without READ/WRITE data authority **when ordinary Linux source eligibility permits it and the open-credential gate is satisfied**. A separately credentialed broker's anonymous tmpfile FD cannot generally be published by an unprivileged receiver via `AT_EMPTY_PATH` in this PoC; the broker must act or another explicitly valid Linux source route must exist. No special-case bypass of that kernel restriction was introduced.
+
+### Runtime-reported bounded result
+
+The new isolated QEMU suite exercised ordinary and `AT_EMPTY_PATH` links, missing/wrong/ancestor scopes, renamed and replacement parents, bind alias, DAC denial positive grant, root `CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE`, CREATE/DELETE/RENAME separation, fresh READ/WRITE denial, symlink follow modes, protected-hardlink denial, O_TMPFILE publication, zero-right clone/dup/SCM_RIGHTS source FDs, and broker credential-gate behavior. It also checked EEXIST, directory-link rejection, EXDEV, and EROFS. The final reported counter was `LINK_TEST_FAILURES=0`. This remains bounded to the exact image and fixture in `/tmp/authority-link`; ACL/idmap, enforcing LSM, races, and callback filesystems remain unqualified.
+
+## 10. Current design forks and decisions
+
+| Question | Rejected alternative | Current choice / evidence | Remaining consequence |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Rights representation | Fixed 64-bit mask | Variable bitmap; right 80 sentinel | PoC class taxonomy remains narrow. |
+| Scope identity | String-prefix path policy | Pinned path, mount+dentry matching | Mount aliases/disconnected paths need more testing. |
+| Traversal | Ambient arbitrary ancestor walk | Bounded handle-first lookup as design direction | Current confine ABI cannot mark TRAVERSE mandatory. |
+| Data write vs unlink | WRITE implies DELETE | Separate rights; parent namespace is target | Directory removal and broader unlink paths remain out. |
+| Rename | DELETE + CREATE compose | Atomic distinct RENAME; both parents | Regular files, flags zero only qualified. |
+| `dup` attenuation | Treat dup as child attenuation | Dup shares open description; clone derives | Existing aliases share later restriction. |
+| Reduced right | Keep hidden re-enable ceiling | No local re-enable from weaker capability | External broker/delegation API is incomplete. |
+| `/proc` reopen | Special-case procfs | Mediate every fresh authority establishment | Unhandled opens/link/import paths remain. |
+| Additive grants | Call additive model a sandbox | Optional mandatory domain for wired rights | Not complete mediation. |
+| DAC future | Remove Unix DAC | Retain compatibility/default DAC; test a narrow provider boundary | Existing regular-file READ/WRITE opens now have a bounded provider experiment; broader VFS integration remains unqualified. |
+| DAC precomputation | Enumerate every DAC-accessible inode as caps | Lazy generic provider from current subject/object/op for existing regular-file READ/WRITE opens | Wider operation and filesystem coverage remains unproven. |
+| CREATE output | CREATE supplies initial data handle | Confined CREATE does not mint READ/WRITE | All capability-producing operations need similar review. |
+| Hard-link publication | CREATE or data rights imply a new name | Exact destination LINK plus normal source route/eligibility | Foreign-credential `AT_EMPTY_PATH` remains subject to Linux `f_cred` gate; other namespace operations remain open. |
+
