+
+## 12. Future RFC framing (not an RFC)
+
+A credible future discussion should not claim “replace Unix permissions,” “replace Landlock/LSMs,” or “new complete Linux security model.” A narrower thesis is:
+
+> Separate ownership/identity from per-process operational filesystem authority while retaining Unix DAC as compatibility/default policy.
+
+Expect questions about why not Landlock, an LSM, or Linux capabilities; why positive grants may override generic DAC; why VFS integration is needed; how arbitrary filesystems behave; what the UAPI guarantees; concrete launcher/broker use cases; and the size of the trusted computing base. The current answer is exploratory: Landlock validates monotonic restriction and semantic rules but is primarily deny-oriented; LSMs remain independent deny/policy layers; DAC remains legacy/default; the prototype explores positive scoped delegation plus a mandatory ceiling. None is claimed to obsolete the others.
+
+## 13. Minimal Authority Core follow-up
+
+The earlier source model had shared authority storage but no common operation-state contract. This follow-up adds an internal request containing a class ID, class-local operation ID, and tagged synchronous context. Each class owns its handled-operation bitmap and domain mode. Selective mode claims only set operations; sealed mode claims every operation in that class. The dispatcher returns exactly three classification states: UNHANDLED, HANDLED+IMPLEMENTED, and HANDLED+UNIMPLEMENTED. UNHANDLED explicitly returns to the caller's legacy Linux path; HANDLED+UNIMPLEMENTED returns `-EOPNOTSUPP` before fallback; implemented requests run the class evaluator. Authorization allow/deny is a subsequent decision, not a fourth classification state.
+
+The existing filesystem UAPI and semantic behavior are retained. Its handled bitmap positions keep their existing numeric meaning; internal filesystem operation IDs map those positions, but `FS_DAC_OVERRIDE` remains a grant rather than an operation. The production filesystem class is selective, and the current UAPI still permits only the previously supported handled operations. No sealing control or new class ABI is added. A CONFIG_KUNIT-only synthetic class tests implemented, explicitly unimplemented, unknown sealed, and unhandled cases, monotonic seal retention, credential-reference inheritance, and confinement-clear behavior. This tests dispatch/lifecycle rules; it does not expose or mediate future classes to userspace.
+
+Filesystem scopes remain path-specific and are evaluated by filesystem-owned code. The request is a tagged union so a later class can add its own typed context and state lifecycle/evaluator hooks rather than treating `struct path` as universal. No process, anonymous-storage, IPC, or network authority is introduced. An absent class is UNHANDLED for compatibility and remains outside domains that did not claim it; the Core is not complete mediation. Exact-source QEMU evidence and limits are in [qualification status](qualification-status.md).
